## Project configuration

The CLI reads project resources from `{{ language.params.executableName|caseLower }}.config.json`. Large resource arrays can be split into smaller JSON files with `includes`:

```json
{
    "projectId": "my-project",
    "includes": {
        "functions": "./appwrite/functions.json",
        "sites": "./appwrite/sites.json",
        "databases": "./appwrite/databases.json",
        "collections": "./appwrite/collections.json",
        "tablesDB": "./appwrite/tables-db.json",
        "tables": "./appwrite/tables.json",
        "buckets": "./appwrite/buckets.json",
        "teams": "./appwrite/teams.json",
        "webhooks": "./appwrite/webhooks.json",
        "topics": "./appwrite/topics.json",
        "messages": "./appwrite/messages.json",
        "analytics": "./appwrite/analytics.json"
    }
}
```

Each included file contains only the array for that resource. Include paths are resolved relative to `{{ language.params.executableName|caseLower }}.config.json`, and resource-local paths inside an included file are resolved relative to that included file:

```json
[
    {
        "$id": "api",
        "name": "API",
        "runtime": "node-22",
        "path": "functions/api",
        "entrypoint": "src/main.ts"
    }
]
```

JSON pointer fragments such as `./resources.json#/functions` are not supported.

### Multiple environments

Pass `--config-file` (or set `APPWRITE_CONFIG_FILE`) to use a config other than the nearest `{{ language.params.executableName|caseLower }}.config.json`, so one repository can target several projects:

```sh
{{ language.params.executableName }} init project --config-file {{ language.params.executableName|caseLower }}.config.prod.json
{{ language.params.executableName }} push --config-file {{ language.params.executableName|caseLower }}.config.prod.json
```

Includes and resource paths are resolved relative to the chosen file.

